# CODE ARCHITECTURE RULES

## 1. Keep responsibilities separated

Use this general separation:

Route Handler
    ↓
Validation
    ↓
Authentication / Authorization
    ↓
Service
    ↓
Repository / Model
    ↓
Database

Route handlers must not become large business-logic files.

---

## 2. Recommended structure

Use this structure where appropriate:

app/
  api/
  (auth)/
  dashboard/
  courses/
  certificates/

components/
  ui/
  layout/
  auth/
  courses/
  player/
  progress/
  certificates/

lib/
  auth/
  db/
  youtube/
  validation/
  errors/
  rate-limit/
  utils/

services/
  auth.service.ts
  course.service.ts
  enrollment.service.ts
  progress.service.ts
  certificate.service.ts
  youtube.service.ts

repositories/
  user.repository.ts
  course.repository.ts
  enrollment.repository.ts

schemas/
  auth.schema.ts
  course.schema.ts
  progress.schema.ts

hooks/
  use-auth.ts
  use-course.ts
  use-progress.ts
  use-youtube-player.ts

---

## 3. Route handlers

Route handlers should primarily:

1. Parse request.
2. Validate input.
3. Authenticate.
4. Authorize.
5. Call service.
6. Return standardized response.

Do not put complex business logic directly inside Route Handlers.

---

## 4. Services

Services contain business rules.

Examples:

- whether a video can be unlocked
- whether progress is valid
- whether a course is complete
- whether a certificate may be generated

Services should be reusable and independently testable.

---

## 5. Database access

Do not scatter raw database operations throughout UI components or route handlers.

Keep MongoDB access centralized.

Use atomic database operations wherever concurrent requests could create inconsistent state.

---

## 6. API response format

Use a consistent response structure.

Success:

{
  "success": true,
  "data": {}
}

Error:

{
  "success": false,
  "error": {
    "code": "ERROR_CODE",
    "message": "User-friendly message"
  }
}

Do not create endpoint-specific response formats unless there is a strong reason.

---

## 7. Configuration

All secrets and environment-specific configuration must come from environment variables.

Never hardcode:

- API keys
- database credentials
- JWT secrets
- storage credentials
- private tokens

Never expose server-only environment variables to client components.

---

## 8. Comments

Comments must explain WHY when the reason is non-obvious.

Do not add comments that simply restate what the code obviously does.

Bad:

// Increment counter
counter++

Good:

// Credit only server-accepted progress so client clock manipulation
// cannot directly increase authoritative watched time.
